WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.054
- Good morning Believe Nation.

00:00:01.054 --> 00:00:03.021
It's Evan and my one word is believe,

00:00:03.021 --> 00:00:05.280
and I believe that you
have something special

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.715
that when you unleash it,

00:00:06.715 --> 00:00:08.761
it will be able to change the world.

00:00:08.761 --> 00:00:11.181
So, to help you on your
journey today's message is

00:00:11.181 --> 00:00:14.015
actively practice gratitude.

00:00:14.015 --> 00:00:15.532
Over to you, Brene Brown.

00:00:15.532 --> 00:00:17.585
(rooster caws)

00:00:17.585 --> 00:00:20.291
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:20.291 --> 00:00:23.238
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:00:23.238 --> 00:00:27.103
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:27.103 --> 00:00:30.982
- What I found, 12 years of research,

00:00:30.982 --> 00:00:32.820
11,000 pieces of data,

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.391
I did not interview in all that time

00:00:36.391 --> 00:00:40.121
a person who would describe
themselves as joyful

00:00:40.121 --> 00:00:42.494
or describe their lives as joyous,

00:00:42.494 --> 00:00:45.827
who did not actively practice gratitude.

00:00:47.665 --> 00:00:49.430
For me it was very counter-intuitive

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:52.096
because I went into the research thinking

00:00:52.096 --> 00:00:54.108
that the relationship between

00:00:54.108 --> 00:00:56.156
joy and gratitude was

00:00:56.156 --> 00:00:59.518
if you're joyful, then
you should be grateful.

00:00:59.518 --> 00:01:01.035
But, it wasn't that way at all.

00:01:01.035 --> 00:01:03.700
It was really that practicing gratitude

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.998
invites joy into our lives.

00:01:05.998 --> 00:01:07.021
When I say practice,

00:01:07.021 --> 00:01:08.824
I think this is the part

00:01:08.824 --> 00:01:10.057
that really changed my life,

00:01:10.057 --> 00:01:13.007
it changed my family and
the way we live everyday.

00:01:13.007 --> 00:01:14.605
When I say practice gratitude,

00:01:14.605 --> 00:01:17.399
I don't mean the attitude of gratitude

00:01:17.399 --> 00:01:18.994
or feeling grateful.

00:01:18.994 --> 00:01:20.754
I mean, practicing gratitude.

00:01:20.754 --> 00:01:23.223
These folks shared in common

00:01:23.223 --> 00:01:26.161
a tangible gratitude practice.

00:01:26.161 --> 00:01:28.954
They either kept gratitude journals,

00:01:28.954 --> 00:01:30.183
some of them did interesting things,

00:01:30.183 --> 00:01:33.336
like at 1,2,3,4 at 12:34 everyday

00:01:33.336 --> 00:01:37.273
they said something out loud
that they were grateful for.

00:01:37.273 --> 00:01:39.318
One of the things that we
do, we say grace at dinner,

00:01:39.318 --> 00:01:41.201
and so now after grace we go around

00:01:41.201 --> 00:01:42.721
and everyone in my family says something

00:01:42.721 --> 00:01:44.372
they're grateful for.

00:01:44.372 --> 00:01:46.199
What's interesting is
when we first started,

00:01:46.199 --> 00:01:48.905
I have a first grader, a
first-grade son Charlie,

00:01:48.905 --> 00:01:50.587
an eighth-grade daughter Ellen,

00:01:50.587 --> 00:01:51.570
and at first I thought,

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:53.048
and we've been doing
it a couple years now,

00:01:53.048 --> 00:01:55.495
they're going to like, "Oh god, Mom."

00:01:55.495 --> 00:01:57.239
There was a little like,

00:01:57.239 --> 00:01:58.299
"Are you experimenting on us?"

00:01:58.299 --> 00:01:59.203
(laughs)

00:01:59.203 --> 00:02:00.560
So, a little bit of that.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:01.667
But, now what's interesting,

00:02:01.667 --> 00:02:04.300
even after we did it
for a couple of weeks,

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:06.067
that on those crazy busy nights

00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:07.953
where we're trying to get to soccer,

00:02:07.953 --> 00:02:09.795
and piano, and homework,

00:02:09.795 --> 00:02:11.680
and Steve and I are just like,

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.279
we say a quick prayer and we start eating,

00:02:13.279 --> 00:02:14.112
and my kids are like,

00:02:14.112 --> 00:02:17.097
"Whoa. What are you grateful for?"

00:02:17.097 --> 00:02:20.212
It's been extraordinary because

00:02:20.212 --> 00:02:21.045
not only

00:02:22.473 --> 00:02:25.712
absolutely does it invite
more joy into our house,

00:02:25.712 --> 00:02:28.702
it also is such a soulful window

00:02:28.702 --> 00:02:30.671
into what's going on in my kids' lives.

00:02:30.671 --> 00:02:33.133
There are some days when my
eighth grader will be like,

00:02:33.133 --> 00:02:35.385
"I'm joyful that there's a huge thick wall

00:02:35.385 --> 00:02:37.230
"between my room and my brother's room."

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:40.865
You know, something very honest.

00:02:40.865 --> 00:02:43.205
But, there are other days she'll say,

00:02:43.205 --> 00:02:46.530
she had a friend whose
mother recently died,

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.277
and for a month she would say,

00:02:49.277 --> 00:02:52.750
"I'm just so grateful that you
all are healthy right now."

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:56.917
Not only did it make us all
more aware of what we had

00:02:57.757 --> 00:02:58.824
and more willing to slow down

00:02:58.824 --> 00:03:02.005
and really be thankful for
the joyful moments we had,

00:03:02.005 --> 00:03:05.158
but it let me know where she
was emotionally in her life.

00:03:05.158 --> 00:03:07.253
My son is always,

00:03:07.253 --> 00:03:08.192
"I'm grateful for bugs.

00:03:08.192 --> 00:03:09.584
"I'm grateful for frogs."

00:03:09.584 --> 00:03:11.592
But, sometimes he'll say,

00:03:11.592 --> 00:03:13.393
"I'm grateful that you
picked me up early,"

00:03:13.393 --> 00:03:15.530
or "I'm grateful that I finally

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:17.530
"understand adjectives."

00:03:19.425 --> 00:03:21.175
There's a great quote

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:23.783
that says,

00:03:23.783 --> 00:03:26.483
"It's not joy that makes us grateful,

00:03:26.483 --> 00:03:29.607
"it's gratitude that makes us joyful."

00:03:29.607 --> 00:03:32.690
It's by a Jesuit priest.

00:03:34.804 --> 00:03:37.596
I guess I was just amazed to find that

00:03:37.596 --> 00:03:39.527
bubble-up so strongly in the research.

00:03:39.527 --> 00:03:41.377
It's life-changing.

00:03:41.377 --> 00:03:42.565
- So, I've had a gratitude practice

00:03:42.565 --> 00:03:44.122
for the past couple of years.

00:03:44.122 --> 00:03:46.707
It started off when I heard,

00:03:46.707 --> 00:03:48.875
I think it was Tony Robbins say,

00:03:48.875 --> 00:03:52.125
that you can't have gratitude and anger

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:55.106
in yourself at the same time.

00:03:55.106 --> 00:03:56.906
If you are really grateful for something,

00:03:56.906 --> 00:03:58.548
then you can't be angry and frustrated

00:03:58.548 --> 00:03:59.654
at the same time,

00:03:59.654 --> 00:04:01.218
and so it's a great medicine

00:04:01.218 --> 00:04:04.295
if you're ever feeling
angry, frustrated, upset,

00:04:04.295 --> 00:04:06.587
then just shifting the perspective

00:04:06.587 --> 00:04:09.095
to just being grateful, asking yourself,

00:04:09.095 --> 00:04:11.062
"What can I be grateful for?"

00:04:11.062 --> 00:04:12.293
If you can actually feel it,

00:04:12.293 --> 00:04:14.102
it takes away the anger, and the pain,

00:04:14.102 --> 00:04:15.415
and the suffering, and the frustration.

00:04:15.415 --> 00:04:17.510
So, I thought it was an interesting test.

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:20.297
I just started by writing
three things down every night.

00:04:20.297 --> 00:04:22.717
I had this little stack of
paper, those tear-aways,

00:04:22.717 --> 00:04:24.437
and I'd write three
things down every night

00:04:24.437 --> 00:04:26.487
that I was grateful for.

00:04:26.487 --> 00:04:30.501
In general, I'm an
optimistic, happy person.

00:04:30.501 --> 00:04:31.766
So, you may think, "Well,
why do you need that?"

00:04:31.766 --> 00:04:33.904
But, I think there's always room

00:04:33.904 --> 00:04:35.875
to try to get better,
to try to be happier,

00:04:35.875 --> 00:04:37.188
to try to feel more fulfilled,

00:04:37.188 --> 00:04:39.761
to look for other hacks and tests.

00:04:39.761 --> 00:04:41.496
Whenever somebody throws
a new thing at me like,

00:04:41.496 --> 00:04:42.358
"Hey, you should try this."

00:04:42.358 --> 00:04:43.834
I'm like, "Okay, let's give it a shot.

00:04:43.834 --> 00:04:44.941
"Let's see how it works.

00:04:44.941 --> 00:04:46.503
"See if it benefits me or not."

00:04:46.503 --> 00:04:48.346
It was something that I kept up doing.

00:04:48.346 --> 00:04:50.937
It was something that I
found some benefit in doing,

00:04:50.937 --> 00:04:53.114
mostly just because of
the perspective shift.

00:04:53.114 --> 00:04:56.154
Just of, one, when I'm
frustrated or angry,

00:04:56.154 --> 00:04:58.696
I can try to think about
what I am grateful for.

00:04:58.696 --> 00:05:00.585
But, two, I would always try

00:05:00.585 --> 00:05:03.209
to find something of my three things

00:05:03.209 --> 00:05:05.421
that was a little different.

00:05:05.421 --> 00:05:06.653
There's the common things like,

00:05:06.653 --> 00:05:08.543
"I'm grateful for this
great country that I'm in,"

00:05:08.543 --> 00:05:09.816
or "I'm grateful for my wife,"

00:05:09.816 --> 00:05:10.886
or "I'm grateful for my son."

00:05:10.886 --> 00:05:14.287
"I'm grateful for my dog
Teemo and his crazy energy."

00:05:14.287 --> 00:05:15.518
But, then I would also always try

00:05:15.518 --> 00:05:18.020
to find something that I would

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:19.784
normally not really notice.

00:05:19.784 --> 00:05:20.975
I'm grateful for this table.

00:05:20.975 --> 00:05:23.064
I'm grateful that it's a
really good height for me.

00:05:23.064 --> 00:05:25.071
I'm grateful that I can
have my books on here.

00:05:25.071 --> 00:05:27.243
I'm grateful that it
looks modern and nice,

00:05:27.243 --> 00:05:29.868
or I'm grateful for that tree outside.

00:05:29.868 --> 00:05:33.155
Just think of things that
normally would go unnoticed

00:05:33.155 --> 00:05:35.246
and finding a way to be grateful for it,

00:05:35.246 --> 00:05:36.928
I found just really

00:05:36.928 --> 00:05:38.626
shifted my perspective a lot.

00:05:38.626 --> 00:05:39.875
So, I've been doing that a couple years.

00:05:39.875 --> 00:05:41.394
I found it really helpful for me.

00:05:41.394 --> 00:05:43.075
I would encourage you to
at least give it a shot

00:05:43.075 --> 00:05:44.512
and see if it works for you.

00:05:44.512 --> 00:05:45.951
The extra step that I took

00:05:45.951 --> 00:05:47.713
that maybe is interesting for you guys

00:05:47.713 --> 00:05:50.047
who are running a team or
have a group of people is

00:05:50.047 --> 00:05:52.310
I created a Facebook
group where I've invited

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:54.358
the people who are on my team

00:05:54.358 --> 00:05:55.878
who want to join in and share,

00:05:55.878 --> 00:05:58.139
and every weekday we post three things

00:05:58.139 --> 00:05:59.202
that we're grateful for

00:05:59.202 --> 00:06:01.417
and one thing that we have learned today.

00:06:01.417 --> 00:06:03.753
It's a daily habit for
the people on my team,

00:06:03.753 --> 00:06:06.789
myself and them, where
we're sharing three things.

00:06:06.789 --> 00:06:08.310
Again, I always try to find one thing

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:10.495
that's a little different,

00:06:10.495 --> 00:06:13.028
like the table or the tree outside.

00:06:13.028 --> 00:06:15.201
Just a little perspective change.

00:06:15.201 --> 00:06:16.556
But, then also it's a great way

00:06:16.556 --> 00:06:17.910
to be able to get to know

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:19.387
the other people on my team better

00:06:19.387 --> 00:06:21.501
and what they're up to.

00:06:21.501 --> 00:06:22.420
When you're having meetings,

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:23.817
you're talking about business and work,

00:06:23.817 --> 00:06:25.050
and you might ask them about their life

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:25.883
and what's going on

00:06:25.883 --> 00:06:27.592
and you learn about important issues,

00:06:27.592 --> 00:06:29.765
but people are posting pictures

00:06:29.765 --> 00:06:31.690
of the camping trip that they took

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:33.287
or the food that they ate,

00:06:33.287 --> 00:06:34.967
and sharing things that

00:06:34.967 --> 00:06:37.510
you wouldn't normally
ask in a conversation.

00:06:37.510 --> 00:06:40.548
It's a great way to
build your team chemistry

00:06:40.548 --> 00:06:42.476
and for people to get to
know each other as well.

00:06:42.476 --> 00:06:46.452
That was a little modification
that I found super helpful.

00:06:46.452 --> 00:06:48.463
And then just one last thought

00:06:48.463 --> 00:06:50.554
just around the perspective shift was

00:06:50.554 --> 00:06:55.053
I used to get really upset in traffic

00:06:55.053 --> 00:06:56.894
and waiting for the elevator.

00:06:56.894 --> 00:06:58.903
Those two things always
really drove me nuts,

00:06:58.903 --> 00:07:00.287
because I don't like wasting time.

00:07:00.287 --> 00:07:03.974
Wasting time is among
my biggest pet peeves,

00:07:03.974 --> 00:07:05.410
and if I'm wasting my time on something,

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:08.325
I look for something
else to do immediately.

00:07:08.325 --> 00:07:09.433
But, when you're stuck in traffic,

00:07:09.433 --> 00:07:10.458
you're waiting for an elevator,

00:07:10.458 --> 00:07:13.240
there's not really a ton of options.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:16.407
Shifting the expectation to gratitude.

00:07:17.379 --> 00:07:19.266
So, "I'm expecting this.

00:07:19.266 --> 00:07:21.236
"I'm expecting to get here quickly."

00:07:21.236 --> 00:07:23.741
It's not happening and
so you get frustrated.

00:07:23.741 --> 00:07:26.449
Shift that expectation
to one of gratitude,

00:07:26.449 --> 00:07:28.301
and just saying, "What
can I be grateful for?"

00:07:28.301 --> 00:07:30.597
So, for the elevator, I'm frustrated like,

00:07:30.597 --> 00:07:31.499
"Where's the elevator?"

00:07:31.499 --> 00:07:34.043
Somebody's moving in and so
there's one less elevator,

00:07:34.043 --> 00:07:35.842
and I'm sitting there maybe with groceries

00:07:35.842 --> 00:07:36.990
and I want to get up.

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:38.298
I just think, "Hm, you know what?

00:07:38.298 --> 00:07:40.226
"There's this magical box

00:07:40.226 --> 00:07:44.038
"that takes me from the basement floor

00:07:44.038 --> 00:07:45.311
"up to the 13th floor

00:07:45.311 --> 00:07:47.113
"where I have tons of groceries in my hand

00:07:47.113 --> 00:07:48.999
"that I don't have to walk the stairs."

00:07:48.999 --> 00:07:51.910
These didn't exist a while ago.

00:07:51.910 --> 00:07:53.961
And just that perspective shift

00:07:53.961 --> 00:07:56.460
allows me to be grateful for the elevator,

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:58.260
and same thing for a car.

00:07:58.260 --> 00:07:59.093
I'm stuck in traffic.

00:07:59.093 --> 00:08:00.757
I'm trying to get somewhere.

00:08:00.757 --> 00:08:02.421
We didn't have cars before.

00:08:02.421 --> 00:08:04.721
We couldn't travel those
kinds of distances before.

00:08:04.721 --> 00:08:06.895
I'd have a much limited life

00:08:06.895 --> 00:08:08.085
if I didn't have the ability

00:08:08.085 --> 00:08:10.483
to get in the car and drive somewhere.

00:08:10.483 --> 00:08:12.472
If I can remember, and
sometimes I need someone

00:08:12.472 --> 00:08:14.527
around me to help shift it.

00:08:14.527 --> 00:08:15.512
I try to tell Nina like,

00:08:15.512 --> 00:08:17.510
"Nina, okay, if you see me frustrated,

00:08:17.510 --> 00:08:18.397
"just remind me.

00:08:18.397 --> 00:08:19.836
"Remind me to be grateful."

00:08:19.836 --> 00:08:22.051
Thinking about it, having
that perspective shift

00:08:22.051 --> 00:08:24.354
immediately takes away the frustration,

00:08:24.354 --> 00:08:27.803
the anger, the resentment,
the expectations,

00:08:27.803 --> 00:08:31.245
and moves me to a much
happier, calm, grateful place.

00:08:31.245 --> 00:08:33.335
I'd encourage you guys to try it out.

00:08:33.335 --> 00:08:36.286
Test it as your own morning
routine or evening routine.

00:08:36.286 --> 00:08:37.394
If it works for you,

00:08:37.394 --> 00:08:38.581
maybe encourage your team members

00:08:38.581 --> 00:08:40.590
to come in as well or friends.

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:42.680
It can be a great way to get
to know each other better

00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:45.633
and keep that motivation
going on a daily basis.

00:08:45.633 --> 00:08:47.521
It might just make a
huge impact on your life.

00:08:47.521 --> 00:08:48.837
Worth a shot at least.

00:08:48.837 --> 00:08:50.314
The question of the day
today is, I'm curious,

00:08:50.314 --> 00:08:52.486
do you have a gratitude practice?

00:08:52.486 --> 00:08:54.295
Is it something that you've
been doing for a while,

00:08:54.295 --> 00:08:55.895
or is it something you are willing

00:08:55.895 --> 00:08:57.577
to test out for the next 10 days

00:08:57.577 --> 00:08:59.706
and see if it has an impact on you or not.

00:08:59.706 --> 00:09:00.811
I love to hear from you.

00:09:00.811 --> 00:09:02.753
Please share your thoughts and experiences

00:09:02.753 --> 00:09:04.271
down in the comment section below.

00:09:04.271 --> 00:09:06.564
I also want to give a quick
shout-out to Yayo Cruz.

00:09:06.564 --> 00:09:08.163
Yayo, thank you so much for picking up

00:09:08.163 --> 00:09:10.129
a copy of my book, Your One Word.

00:09:10.129 --> 00:09:11.485
I hope you enjoy the read,

00:09:11.485 --> 00:09:13.125
and I really appreciate your support.

00:09:13.125 --> 00:09:14.440
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:15.588
I believe in you.

00:09:15.588 --> 00:09:17.561
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself.

00:09:17.561 --> 00:09:19.531
Whatever your one word is,

00:09:19.531 --> 00:09:21.416
much love and I'll see you guys again

00:09:21.416 --> 00:09:25.160
tomorrow morning for
another shot of Entspresso.

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:27.573
♫ I wake up every morning

00:09:27.573 --> 00:09:31.156
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:09:34.175 --> 00:09:35.243
- The next phase here is also

00:09:35.243 --> 00:09:38.258
to become grateful for what
it is that you do have.

00:09:38.258 --> 00:09:39.461
So, if you're starting to try

00:09:39.461 --> 00:09:40.813
to create change in your life,

00:09:40.813 --> 00:09:42.698
you want to make sure that
you're really focusing

00:09:42.698 --> 00:09:45.239
on what it is that you
do have in your life,

00:09:45.239 --> 00:09:48.849
so that you feel an energy of gratitude.

00:09:48.849 --> 00:09:51.885
An energy of gratitude
can really open you up

00:09:51.885 --> 00:09:55.620
to this incredible capacity
to feel more energized,

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:57.709
to feel more happy.

00:09:57.709 --> 00:09:59.308
In any given moment where you feel like,

00:09:59.308 --> 00:10:00.579
"Oh, I don't have what I need,"

00:10:00.579 --> 00:10:01.683
or "I'm not supported,"

00:10:01.683 --> 00:10:03.075
or things aren't working out,

00:10:03.075 --> 00:10:05.124
the simple step here is to just say,

00:10:05.124 --> 00:10:07.995
"I am grateful for what I do have."

00:10:07.995 --> 00:10:08.938
When we look and when we say,

00:10:08.938 --> 00:10:10.333
"I'm grateful for what I do have,"

00:10:10.333 --> 00:10:12.916
we create more of what we want.

00:10:14.395 --> 00:10:17.802
The energy of gratitude opens our heart

00:10:17.802 --> 00:10:19.281
and it expands our energy,

00:10:19.281 --> 00:10:22.076
and it makes us a much more wonderful,

00:10:22.076 --> 00:10:24.329
loving person to be around.

00:10:24.329 --> 00:10:25.475
With that loving energy,

00:10:25.475 --> 00:10:28.685
we begin to attract more into our life.

00:10:28.685 --> 00:10:29.873
We want to really make sure that

00:10:29.873 --> 00:10:32.706
that energy of gratitude is a very

00:10:33.731 --> 00:10:37.311
pentacle part of this
miracle-worker process,

00:10:37.311 --> 00:10:41.053
which is that we stay committed and open

00:10:41.053 --> 00:10:43.469
to what it is that we are here to receive.

00:10:43.469 --> 00:10:46.216
And we stay committed and grateful

00:10:46.216 --> 00:10:48.549
for what it is that we have.

00:10:49.460 --> 00:10:50.890
You can begin today just by making

00:10:50.890 --> 00:10:53.225
a gratitude list and focus on it.

00:10:53.225 --> 00:10:54.457
There's so many moments when I think,

00:10:54.457 --> 00:10:55.766
"Oh my god. I can't
believe I have to do this,"

00:10:55.766 --> 00:10:57.369
or "I can't believe I
have to get to that,"

00:10:57.369 --> 00:10:59.116
then I just think about
what I'm grateful for,

00:10:59.116 --> 00:11:02.150
and it just spins me immediately
out of the crazy chaos

00:11:02.150 --> 00:11:04.995
and back into a centered place of love.

00:11:04.995 --> 00:11:06.853
I think that this is
a really valuable tool

00:11:06.853 --> 00:11:08.903
for you to begin to use regularly,

00:11:08.903 --> 00:11:10.665
and it's an active tool
throughout the book,

00:11:10.665 --> 00:11:12.142
May Cause Miracles.

00:11:12.142 --> 00:11:13.416
- If you're obsessed,
you know what you want,

00:11:13.416 --> 00:11:14.688
you take massive action,

00:11:14.688 --> 00:11:16.658
maybe you modeled somebody who
figured out the right action,

00:11:16.658 --> 00:11:19.651
so you get there quicker,
and you're executing,

00:11:19.651 --> 00:11:21.534
you still need a little grace.

00:11:21.534 --> 00:11:23.342
Some people call it luck,

00:11:23.342 --> 00:11:25.004
you could call it God,

00:11:25.004 --> 00:11:26.967
but I really believe that
there's the part we do

00:11:26.967 --> 00:11:28.767
and then there's the
part that comes to us.

00:11:28.767 --> 00:11:30.205
But, I do know

00:11:30.205 --> 00:11:31.849
grace shows up a lot more when you do

00:11:31.849 --> 00:11:33.528
the first two steps on your own,

00:11:33.528 --> 00:11:35.372
and grace shows up a lot more

00:11:35.372 --> 00:11:36.728
when you acknowledge grace in your life.

00:11:36.728 --> 00:11:38.985
When you acknowledge that
I was born in a world where

00:11:38.985 --> 00:11:40.504
I didn't have to build this internet

00:11:40.504 --> 00:11:42.143
that I can pull out of my pocket

00:11:42.143 --> 00:11:43.289
to answer anything on earth,

00:11:43.289 --> 00:11:45.061
I'm driving on streets I didn't pave,

00:11:45.061 --> 00:11:47.446
I'm reading books I didn't have to write.

00:11:47.446 --> 00:11:48.551
That's the gift of our lives,

00:11:48.551 --> 00:11:50.477
and I think when we experience that grace,

00:11:50.477 --> 00:11:52.365
we don't just experience more achievement,

00:11:52.365 --> 00:11:54.091
but we start to experience
more fulfillment.

00:11:54.091 --> 00:11:56.223
- Grace not only affects
your ability to get there,

00:11:56.223 --> 00:11:57.373
but it affects what you do

00:11:57.373 --> 00:11:59.831
and how you live once you are there.

00:11:59.831 --> 00:12:02.242
- It really determines the
quality of your life because

00:12:02.242 --> 00:12:05.746
unless you live in a state of gratitude...

00:12:05.746 --> 00:12:08.495
I did an interview with Sir John Templeton

00:12:08.495 --> 00:12:11.120
who, as you know, is one of
the first billionaire investors

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:12.065
and just a brilliant man.

00:12:12.065 --> 00:12:15.139
He came from nothing and he donated,

00:12:15.139 --> 00:12:17.228
I think it was 750, they still
do it, the Templeton Fund.

00:12:17.228 --> 00:12:18.743
I think it's $750 million a year.

00:12:18.743 --> 00:12:22.433
It's bigger than a Nobel
fund that he donates.

00:12:22.433 --> 00:12:23.334
One day I asked him, I said,

00:12:23.334 --> 00:12:25.670
"What is the secret to wealth?"

00:12:25.670 --> 00:12:27.064
And he looked at me, paused.

00:12:27.064 --> 00:12:29.162
This big smile and his
eyes got really bright

00:12:29.162 --> 00:12:31.377
like they always did, and he said,

00:12:31.377 --> 00:12:32.976
"Tony, it's what you teach."

00:12:32.976 --> 00:12:34.413
I said, "Well, I teach a
lot of things. (laughs)

00:12:34.413 --> 00:12:36.832
"Which thing?" He said, "Gratitude."

00:12:36.832 --> 00:12:38.511
He said, "Because you and I Tony

00:12:38.511 --> 00:12:41.009
"know billionaires that are miserable

00:12:41.009 --> 00:12:42.735
"and it's because they're not
grateful for what they have."

00:12:42.735 --> 00:12:45.486
We both know people have
no money, seemingly,

00:12:45.486 --> 00:12:47.127
but they're so grateful for their health,

00:12:47.127 --> 00:12:48.929
for their husband, for their
wife, for their children.

00:12:48.929 --> 00:12:49.828
They're so grateful to God,

00:12:49.828 --> 00:12:53.995
they're so grateful for
their life that they're rich.

00:12:53.995 --> 00:12:55.492
I think it starts with that gratitude.

00:12:55.492 --> 00:12:57.092
It starts with that connection to grace.

00:12:57.092 --> 00:12:58.201
As simplistic as that sounds,

00:12:58.201 --> 00:13:00.254
and so it's not enough to
have your financial plan.

00:13:00.254 --> 00:13:01.900
You have all the money and be miserable.

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:03.705
Let's have all the
money and be really rich

00:13:03.705 --> 00:13:05.675
emotionally and psychologically as well.

00:13:05.675 --> 00:13:07.931
- Finally, waking up every morning

00:13:07.931 --> 00:13:11.178
and figuring out, even
in a difficult situation,

00:13:11.178 --> 00:13:13.314
where the pieces of gratitude are.

00:13:13.314 --> 00:13:15.204
So, like coming into the city

00:13:15.204 --> 00:13:17.724
yesterday or the day before

00:13:17.724 --> 00:13:19.784
I was stuck in traffic and
I was late for something.

00:13:19.784 --> 00:13:22.323
I was like, "Ugh, you got to go faster."

00:13:22.323 --> 00:13:24.486
But, this is an opportunity

00:13:24.486 --> 00:13:28.244
to solve a difficult
gratitude problem, which is

00:13:28.244 --> 00:13:31.042
it's the city I'm going to
is so amazing and wonderful

00:13:31.042 --> 00:13:33.901
that everyone else wants to
be here even faster than me.

